%I A401290 #24 Nov 04 2026 15:35:59
%S A401290 1,2,4,8,16,30,54,92,152,242,378,576,868,1286,1894,2756,4000,5754,
%T A401290 8274,11816,16892,24014,34206,48492,68920,97538,138442,195728,277588,
%U A401290 392214,555990,785300,1112912,1571594,2226882,3144312,4454956,6289886,8911246,12581180,17823976,25163922
%N A401290 Number of green squares after n unfoldings in the edge-memory unfolding array.
%C A401290 Start with one green square. At each step, double the paper by unfolding alternately downward and rightward. Every existing square produces a new square in its mirror position. A newborn square is green if its parent has ever been on the outer edge of the paper; otherwise, it is orange. Colors never change, and each square remembers whether it has ever been on an edge. a(n) is the number of green squares after n unfoldings, with a(0) = 1.
%H A401290 M. F. Hasler, <a href="/A401290/b401290.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 0..1000</a>, Oct 19 2026.
%H A401290 Ali Sada, <a href="/A401290/a401290.jpg">Illustration of the first ten unfoldings</a>, Oct 19 2026.
%F A401290 a(n) = 2^n - b(n), where b(n+1) = A027556(n-1) + 2*b(n) - b(n-1) for n > 0, b(n) = 0 for n < 5. - _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 19 2026
%e A401290 After four unfoldings, the paper is a 4 by 4 square containing 16 green squares. At the fifth unfolding, two offspring are orange because their parents have never been on an edge. The other 14 offspring are green. Therefore, a(5) = 16+14 = 30.
%o A401290 (Python)
%o A401290 def A401290(n):
%o A401290     if not hasattr(A:=A401290,'b'): A.b = [0]*5
%o A401290     while (L:=len(A.b)) <= n:
%o A401290         A.b.append((2**(L-3>>1)-1)*(2**(L-2>>1)-1)*2+2*A.b[-1]-A.b[-2])
%o A401290     return 2**n-A.b[n] # _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 19 2026
%Y A401290 Cf. A000079 (2^n), A027556 (unbalanced strings of length n).
%Y A401290 Cf. A401293 (number of orange squares).
%K A401290 nonn,easy,new
%O A401290 0,2
%A A401290 _Ali Sada_, Oct 19 2026
%E A401290 More terms from _M. F. Hasler_, Oct 19 2026
